        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing powders on protein per scoop alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elevation's 30 g comes from a large 44 to 49 g serving. A powder listing 24 g in a 31 g scoop can be more concentrated. Divide protein by serving weight, or compare cost per 25 g of protein, before deciding which tub gives you more for your money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting on the powder for your creatine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older Elevation tubs advertised added creatine inside a blend, with no clear dose. Effective creatine intake is about 3 to 5 g a day. If you want creatine, buy plain creatine monohydrate separately so you know exactly what you are taking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the website over the tub on allergens</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Aldi markets current tubs as gluten free, but a database entry also lists wheat and egg. Formulas and facility statements change. Anyone with celiac disease or a wheat or egg allergy should read the label on the actual tub before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a store brand as sport-safe</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Elevation has no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport listing we could find. That is fine for gym-goers, but athletes in drug-tested sports carry the risk of a contaminated supplement themselves. Choose a certified powder and check the certifier's database.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the calories when cutting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At 170 to 180 calories per serving, Elevation is heavier than a lean isolate. Mixed with milk, a shake can pass 300 calories. That is fine for bulking, but during fat loss mix it with water and count it like any other food.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

