          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Elevation protein powder good?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people who tolerate dairy, yes. It uses a whey concentrate and isolate blend, gives about 30 g of protein per serving per the label and keeps sugar to around 2 g. The trade-offs are artificial sweeteners, a large serving, no sport certification and no published lab testing. As a budget whey it does what it promises.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Aldi protein powder healthy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a reasonable food for healthy adults trying to eat more protein. Whey is a high-quality complete protein, and Elevation is low in sugar. It does contain sucralose, acesulfame potassium, gums and flavorings. People with kidney disease, pregnancy, allergies or regular medication should check protein targets with a doctor or registered dietitian first.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Elevation protein powder have creatine?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Older Elevation tubs and listings advertised added amino acids and creatine, but reviewers report the formula was later simplified, and the current whey blend listings we found do not mention creatine. Check your tub. Even where creatine was listed, the dose was not clear, so take creatine monohydrate separately if you want its benefits.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Elevation protein powder gluten free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Aldi markets the current Elevation whey tubs as gluten free on aldi.us. One nutrition database entry also lists wheat and egg, likely from a shared-facility statement on an older version. Because labels change, anyone with celiac disease or a wheat allergy should read the allergen statement on the tub they actually buy.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Elevation protein powder have lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We are not aware of any published heavy-metal testing for Elevation, from Aldi or independent labs, so there is no data either way. Whey generally tests lower for heavy metals than plant and chocolate-heavy powders. If this matters to you, choose a brand that publishes batch results, or pick vanilla over chocolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Who makes Elevation protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Aldi does not name the manufacturer, and we found no reliable source that does. That is standard for store brands, including Costco's Kirkland whey. It means you rely on Aldi's supplier standards and its return guarantee rather than a brand's public testing record, which is fine for most buyers but not for tested athletes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Elevation protein powder safe?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For healthy adults using it as directed, there is no specific safety concern we are aware of. It is a mainstream whey made from common, permitted ingredients. It lacks independent certification and published testing, so tested athletes should avoid it. People with a milk allergy must avoid it, and anyone with a medical condition should ask a clinician.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

